WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.080
You're sitting in a café and watching a huge queue form.

2
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:09.120
People come in who are on a work break and want to order themselves a coffee.

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.060
You think that everyone is probably stressed,

4
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.300
because now their whole break will go to standing in line for coffee,

5
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.040
and you decide to investigate this problem.

6
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.340
Hi, folks, today we're talking about how to learn as much as possible from your interviews

7
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.240
and how to structure the data you collect.

8
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.180
So, let's get started.

9
00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.300
Let's go back to the previous example.

10
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.840
Now think about which people you could talk to about this problem.

11
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.480
Who might have this problem at all?

12
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.400
Who gets really annoyed by having to stand in a long line.

13
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.420
And so you find 10 people you'll interview.

14
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.360
Okay, you conduct the interview, but during the interview you have very little time.

15
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.260
The only thing you manage is to quickly jot down some things you hear,

16
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.460
and write down as much as possible, but then this format turns out to be hard to read.

17
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.800
And to draw some conclusions from these interviews,

18
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.700
now you need to somehow structure this data in order to learn something new.

19
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.700
One of the techniques UX researchers use to do this is the empathy map.

20
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.280
So that you understand as well as possible what an empathy map is,

21
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.100
I'll show you with a practical example.

22
00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:20.400
For this example I'll use Figma, but it's important to remember that it isn't necessary.

23
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.660
You can make an empathy map on paper too.

24
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:28.020
First, in your browser you type "Figma," and if you don't have an account, you first create an account,

25
00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.280
and once you've created an account, you'll be taken to a start page like this.

26
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:38.000
In the FigJam and Templates section you can choose Empathy Map

27
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.520
and click Use Template.

28
00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:48.860
Now Figma will open this template for you, and on the left you have basic instructions.

29
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.380
Next to them is Persona information,

30
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.420
which is information about the person you're interviewing.

31
00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.420
And next to that is the empathy map.

32
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:03.940
It's important to remember that not every source's empathy map looks the same,

33
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:09.220
that is, some have four sections, some six, depending on what's convenient for them.

34
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.880
In Figma you get six sections,

35
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.680
but in the example I'll show you, I used four sections.

36
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.080
Now I'll move on to the example I worked through.

37
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:28.100
Let's say that for our recent problem we interviewed Sara Sarić.

38
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:35.140
Sara Sarić is 27 years old, she lives in a big city, let's say Belgrade.

39
00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.920
She has a busy work schedule, always rushing around, working a lot.

40
00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.300
And Sara cares about health and fitness.

41
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:51.520
So, on the right you can see roughly my notes

42
00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.260
that I managed to take while I was talking to Sara.

43
00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.840
And now I'll read them to you.

44
00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.220
During the interview.

45
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.820
So, my empathy map consists of four sections.

46
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.280
The first section is "says," the second section is "thinks,"

47
00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.120
the third section is "does," and the fourth section is "feels."

48
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.320
In the middle you can see a small picture of Sara, so that she's kind of in focus.

49
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.880
Now we'll go through each section step by step

50
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.800
and explain what each section should contain.

51
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:59.100
Empathy maps are usually made using those very sticky notes.

52
00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:04.060
That is, here in Figma you drag them in, and if you were using paper,

53
00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:08.740
you'd buy a small pack of sticky notes and stick them in each of the fields.

54
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.500
So, the first section is "says."

55
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:20.260
In the "says" section you need to write on sticky notes everything that Sara told us

56
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.760
and that for some reason seemed significant to us.

57
00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.080
That is, something that caught our attention more.

58
00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.800
On my sticky notes in the "says" section I put what

59
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.640
Sara said: she uses an app.

60
00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:40.660
The second thing Sara said is that she likes simplicity.

61
00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:47.010
Then, Sara also said that she likes homemade coffee.

62
00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:55.310
And the fourth thing that stuck with me, that seemed significant to me, is

63
00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:57.870
that she's careful not to spill the coffee.

64
00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:01.250
Your sticky notes may look different,

65
00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:08.030
but this is how I understood our conversation and what seemed important to me.

66
00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:13.290
The second section is the "thinks" section, and you can notice that compared to the first section

67
00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:17.270
I'm using a different color of sticky notes, to somehow separate them.

68
00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:25.150
You can choose a different sticky note color in Figma simply with this arrow next to the sticky note.

69
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:32.370
And in the "thinks" section you need to write what Sara's beliefs are,

70
00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:37.270
that is, what she said implicitly.

71
00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:42.790
That is, she didn't tell you specifically, but you could draw a conclusion based on what she told you.

72
00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:47.430
So, to make it easier to explain, I'll go through examples.

73
00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:52.390
So, Sara said that it's important for her to have physical activity during the day.

74
00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:53.970
How did I draw that conclusion?

75
00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:59.090
Well, Sara said she bikes to work because on workdays she doesn't have time to go to the gym.

76
00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:02.670
So from that I concluded that physical activity is important to her.

77
00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:08.070
The second thing I drew a conclusion about — she believes she needs coffee as soon as she gets up.

78
00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:12.450
That is, Sara said that every day before work she first goes to get coffee.

79
00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:16.910
And the third thing I drew a conclusion about — she believes she has no time.

80
00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:23.590
That is, when Sara was saying why she doesn't go to the nearest café but gets coffee at Starbucks,

81
00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:27.010
she said that then she doesn't have to stand in line.

82
00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.670
Okay, once we've filled in the first two sections,

83
00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.510
we move on to the third section, the "does" section.

84
00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:35.610
That is, what does Sara say she does?

85
00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.350
Well, in this section I highlighted three things.

86
00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:43.590
So, Sara told us that she bikes, that is, she rides her bike when she gets to work.

87
00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:48.690
Sara told us that she drinks coffee, buys coffee every day before work.

88
00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:53.510
And Sara told us that she uses her phone a lot.

89
00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:58.110
Now, she didn't say this directly, but you can somehow conclude it,

90
00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:03.710
because she has a separate app that she uses only to order herself coffee before work.

91
00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:09.170
Now that we've finished this third section, "does," we move on to the fourth and last section,

92
00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:11.010
and that's the "feels" section.

93
00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:15.350
These are the feelings Sara experiences when she faces this problem.

94
00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:21.790
The feelings I highlighted here: Sara feels rushed,

95
00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:24.350
because every day she's hurrying to work.

96
00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:29.850
Then, Sara is impatient, because she has no time to stand in line.

97
00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:36.770
And Sara is determined, because she still bikes to work,

98
00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:40.490
even if she's late, just so she gets some physical activity during the day.

99
00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:45.830
Now that we've neatly sorted all this into these four segments, or four sections,

100
00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:49.710
we can look for what our new conclusions are,

101
00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:54.750
or how Sara copes with the problem we're investigating.

102
00:07:56.010 --> 00:08:00.670
Well, we can see that Sara really does have this problem,

103
00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.170
that is, before she probably stood in line,

104
00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:06.810
but found a solution for herself.

105
00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:10.710
She found a solution in the form of the Starbucks app,

106
00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:13.730
but is still not completely satisfied.

107
00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:16.870
Why? Because Sara said that she likes homemade coffee.

108
00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.150
And probably, if there were some solution

109
00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:22.170
that would let her drink homemade coffee,

110
00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:23.970
but without standing in line,

111
00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.290
she would probably use it.

112
00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:29.970
In addition, we learned some other new and interesting things.

113
00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.950
For example, that Sara uses apps.

114
00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:36.170
That is, it's no problem for her to download an app to her phone.

115
00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.070
On top of that we learned that health matters

116
00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:42.950
and that physical activity matters, since she bikes to work.

117
00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:46.070
And the interesting insights I'd highlight —

118
00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.990
it's that she said she likes simplicity

119
00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:50.670
and that she's careful not to spill the coffee.

120
00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:53.230
So it's important that the solution be simple,

121
00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.470
and when she says she's careful not to spill the coffee,

122
00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:59.630
that might suggest to you that maybe at some point she had a problem,

123
00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.510
that she used to spill coffee like that.

124
00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:04.450
And so now she pays a bit more attention to it.

125
00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:07.130
This is an example of how you can use an empathy map

126
00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:09.450
to learn more from your interviews

127
00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:13.370
and to somehow structure the information you've gathered.

128
00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:17.470
We need empathy maps to learn more from our interviews.

129
00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:21.170
How our respondents actually deal with this problem,

130
00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:23.650
or whether they already have some existing solutions

131
00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:26.710
and what their impressions of those existing solutions currently are.

132
00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:30.350
An empathy map doesn't have to be created separately for each user.

133
00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.650
You can group similar users

134
00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:36.110
and make just one empathy map based on that.

135
00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:40.070
Also, empathy maps evolve along with our users.

136
00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:40.930
What does that mean?

137
00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:44.750
Well, if some new solution for their problem appears on the market,

138
00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:50.150
maybe their habits, that is, the way they solve this problem for themselves, will change.

139
00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:53.610
And you'll have to change your empathy map too.

140
00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:57.670
This is just one way to structure the data

141
00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.010
you got from the interviews,

142
00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:02.030
and it's one of the design thinking techniques.

143
00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:06.250
If you have some other way to organize your research

144
00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:07.910
and it's maybe better than this one,

145
00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:09.770
write to us below in the comments,

146
00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:11.950
and also, it would be important to us

147
00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:15.710
if you told us whether you're interested in more design thinking topics

148
00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.290
and whether we should cover more of them soon.

149
00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:20.470
And that's all from me for today.

150
00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.250
See you next time. Bye!